'''''Carex laevigata''''', the '''smooth-stalked sedge''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> is a species of [[Carex|sedge]]. It lives in moist, shady environment in the [[lowland]]s of [[Western Europe|Western]] and [[Central Europe]], particularly in [[British NVC community W7|alder–ash woodland]].<ref name="Jermy">{{cite book |author1=A. C. Jermy |author2=D. A. Simpson |author3=M. J. Y. Foley |author4=M. S. Porter |year=2007 |title=Sedges of the British Isles |edition=3rd |series=BSBI Handbook No. 1 |isbn=978-0-901158-35-2 |publisher=[[Botanical Society of the British Isles]] |chapter=''Carex laevigata'' Sm. |pages=356–357}}</ref> It is distinguished from similar species, such as ''[[Carex binervis|C. binervis]]'' and ''[[Carex distans|C. distans]]'' by the presence of tiny red dots on the [[utricle (fruit)|utricles]].<ref name="Jermy"/> ''Carex laevigata'' was [[species description|first described]] by [[James Edward Smith (botanist)|James Edward Smith]] in 1800, in a paper in the journal ''[[Transactions of the Linnean Society of London]]''.<ref name="Smith">{{cite journal |author=James Edward Smith |author-link=James Edward Smith (botanist) |year=1800 |title=Descriptions of five new British species of ''Carex'' |journal=[[Transactions of the Linnean Society of London]] |volume=5 |issue=19 |pages=264–273 |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/46568#page/312/mode/1up |doi=10.1111/j.1096-3642.1800.tb00600.x}}</ref>

